Here's a function that performs the described operations:

```javascript
function updateRatingButtons(currentRating, newRating) {
    // Convert values to integers and multiply by 100
    const currentRatingValue = Math.round(currentRating * 100);
    const newRatingValue = Math.round(newRating * 100);

    // Create arrays to store rating values
    const currentRatingArray = Array(5).fill(0).map((_, i) => currentRatingValue - (i * 25));
    const newRatingArray = Array(5).fill(0).map((_, i) => newRatingValue - (i * 25));

    // Create a tuple array to store changes
    const changes = Array(5).fill(0).map((_, i) => [currentRatingArray[i], newRatingArray[i], currentRatingArray[i] !== newRatingArray[i]]);

    // Update rating buttons
    ratingButtons.forEach((btn, index) => {
        const [current, newRating, changed] = changes[index];
        if (changed) {
            btn.classList.remove(...btn.classList.filter(cls => cls !== `rating-${current}`));
            btn.classList.add(`rating-${newRating}`);
        }
    });
}
```

This function assumes that `ratingButtons` is a NodeList or an array of HTML elements representing the rating buttons. The `ratingButtons` should have classes like `rating-0`, `rating-25`, `rating-50`, `rating-75`, and `rating-100` to reflect their current state

